// THE ONE THING STANDING BETWEEN M4 AND ITS SIX CONSUMERS, refused out
// loud rather than answered quietly (`docs/design_meta.md` §5, §7 M4).
//
// A TRAIT IS A STRUCT (`DESIGN.md`: "a struct whose fields happen to be
// functions, used as a bound"), so `Display.dump`'s body is written
// among `Display`'s own members and every implementor gets a copy of
// it. `@Self` there is the BOUND, not the implementor — sema binds it
// once, because §5 says a body is checked once and an `@meta` body is
// the first for which that is wrong.
//
// SO THE WALK, UNROLLED ANYWAY, READS THE BOUND'S OWN MEMBERS. Measured
// before this refusal existed: `Dumpy` declares one field and one
// method, `P` declares two fields, and `P(a: 1, b: "x").show()` printed
// `tally` — the trait's field, for a type that has no such field. The
// version with no fields on the trait printed NOTHING AT ALL, compiled
// clean, and exited 0. That is the failure §6.4 names — "a walk that
// silently skips a field prints something plausible" — arriving with
// every field skipped.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT LEGAL AGAIN is expansion PER INSTANTIATION rather than
// once, which M4 does not build and M4's report names as its debt. A
// walk over a NAMED type is unaffected, and so is `@Self` inside an
// `impl` body, where it is one concrete type.
//
// position: the `@meta` token.

Dumpy = {
    tally*: i64,

    show* = (self: @Self) () {
        @meta(self: @Self).fields().loop((h, field) {
            println("{}", field.name);
        });
    }
}

P = { a*: i64, b*: str }

P.impl(Dumpy, { tally: 9 })

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    P(a: 1, b: "x").show();
    Ok(0);
}
